        <!-- Article Body -->
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">An active harmonic filter is only as good as its control loop. The filter must detect harmonic current, compute an anti-phase reference, and switch IGBTs or SiC MOSFETs before the load current has moved on — all within a fraction of a mains cycle. In CHITEK active harmonic filter design, that loop runs on an FPGA, and the choice of silicon sets the ceiling on what the loop can do. This article covers the control chain, the timing budget, why DSP-based designs fall short, and how to specify and commission an FPGA-controlled AHF alongside a static var generator.</p>
<h2 id="what-the-control-loop-must-do" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">What the Control Loop Must Do</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Every AHF executes three tasks in sequence, and each one consumes time from the same budget:</p>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Harmonic detection</strong> — extract the harmonic content from the measured load current using FFT or instantaneous power theory (pq theory).</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Reference generation</strong> — invert that content to produce the compensation current command i_c*.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">PWM synthesis</strong> — switch the power bridge so the output current tracks i_c* within the ripple band.</li></ul>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The command itself is simple to state:</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">i_c* = i_L - i_s(fundamental)</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The difficulty is that i_L changes continuously. A rectifier front end on a variable-speed drive produces a current step at every commutation, and the filter has roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">one switching period</strong> to respond before the error integrates into visible distortion.</p>
<h2 id="how-fpga-control-is-quantified" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">How FPGA Control Is Quantified</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The metric that matters is loop latency: the time from a change in load current to a corresponding change in filter output current. In a DSP-based design, sampling, interrupt entry, sequential computation, and PWM update typically stack into tens of microseconds. An FPGA executes the detection and reference math in parallel hardware, so latency drops to the <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">microsecond</strong> range.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">That margin is what allows the filter to track the 5th, 7th, 11th, and 13th harmonics — the dominant orders from a six-pulse rectifier — without phase lag at the switching frequency. Latency also sets the achievable control bandwidth, and bandwidth sets how fast the loop can reject a disturbance.</p>
<h2 id="major-causes-of-poor-harmonic-compensation" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Major Causes of Poor Harmonic Compensation</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">When an AHF underperforms on site, the cause is usually one of these:</p>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Control latency</strong> — a slow loop leaves a residual error that shows up as elevated THDi at the PCC.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Detection error</strong> — a PLL that loses lock during voltage distortion misidentifies the fundamental and misallocates harmonic content.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">PWM timing jitter</strong> — imprecise gate timing widens the output current ripple and wastes switching headroom.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Saturation</strong> — the filter runs out of current capacity during load peaks and stops compensating.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Resonance with existing capacitors</strong> — the filter and a nearby bank interact, producing amplification rather than cancellation.</li></ul>
<h2 id="what-it-costs" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">What It Costs</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Utility penalties</strong> — IEEE 519 and GB/T limits on voltage and current distortion are enforced at the PCC; a residual THDi above the limit is a recurring charge.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Transformer and cable heating</strong> — harmonic current raises I²R losses and derates capacity.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Capacitor failure</strong> — harmonic-rich voltage accelerates dielectric aging in power factor correction banks.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Nuisance tripping</strong> — distorted voltage confuses protection relays and drives.</li></ul>
<h2 id="why-dsp-based-control-falls-short" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Why DSP-Based Control Falls Short</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A DSP runs the detection algorithm, the reference calculation, and the PWM update in sequence. Each step waits for the previous one, and each interrupt adds overhead. The result is a loop that is fast enough for slowly varying loads but lags behind a drive that switches every few hundred microseconds.</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Raising the DSP clock helps only marginally, because the bottleneck is architectural, not clock speed. A faster processor still executes one instruction at a time. Moving the same math into FPGA fabric removes the serialization entirely.</p>
<h2 id="how-fpga-control-fixes-it" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">How FPGA Control Fixes It</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The FPGA partitions the control chain into parallel hardware blocks that all run in the same clock cycle. Detection, reference generation, and PWM comparison proceed simultaneously rather than in turn.</p>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Parallel detection</strong> — FFT or pq-theory blocks process all phases at once, with no shared execution resource.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Deterministic PWM</strong> — gate signals are generated in hardware, so timing jitter is bounded by the clock, not by software scheduling.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Closed-loop feedback</strong> — the measured output current is compared against i_c* every switching period, and the error is corrected before it accumulates.</li></ul>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">In CHITEK active harmonic filter design, this architecture pulls THDi from <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">30%+ down to &lt; 5%</strong> at the PCC, with the compensation tracking the load within <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">&lt; 1 ms</strong>. The same control platform drives the CHITEK SVG, where per-phase reactive current injection holds power factor at <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">0.99+</strong> and rebalances negative-sequence current that a fixed bank cannot touch.</p>
<h2 id="igbt-vs-sic" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">IGBT vs SiC</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The control loop can only exploit the switching speed the power stage allows. Conventional IGBT-based AHF and static var generator units are capped near <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">150 °C</strong> junction temperature and switch typically below <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">20 kHz</strong>. SiC MOSFETs raise the junction limit to <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">200 °C</strong>, offer roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">10×</strong> the breakdown field, and support <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">2-3×</strong> the switching frequency with about <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">50%</strong> lower switching loss.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6"><table class="w-full border-collapse"><thead><tr class="bg-gray-50"><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">Parameter</th><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">IGBT stage</th><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">SiC MOSFET stage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Junction temperature</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~150 °C</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~200 °C</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Typical switching frequency</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">&lt; 20 kHz</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">2-3× higher</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Switching loss</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Baseline</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~50% lower</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Control bandwidth headroom</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Limited</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Wide</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The practical effect is a faster inner loop, a smaller output filter, and a narrower cabinet for the same compensation current. That is the direction CHITEK's LV AHF + SVG platform is built on.</p>
<h2 id="engineering-deployment-checklist" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Engineering Deployment Checklist</h2>
<ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li>Measure load current and PCC voltage over a full duty cycle; log the harmonic spectrum and the worst-case THDi.</li><li>Confirm the dominant harmonic orders and size the filter current to cover them with <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">15-25%</strong> headroom.</li><li>Verify the control loop latency against the fastest load step expected on site.</li><li>Audit existing capacitor banks for resonance before commissioning.</li><li>Check weak-grid margin and confirm the filter holds regulation through the worst-case voltage dip.</li><li>After commissioning, re-measure THDi at the PCC against IEEE 519 and GB/T limits.</li></ol>
<h2 id="typical-application-profiles" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Typical Application Profiles</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Variable-speed drive clusters</strong> — commutation steps every few hundred microseconds; FPGA latency keeps the residual error small.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Data centers</strong> — switch-mode power supplies generate broad-spectrum harmonics that need parallel detection across many orders.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Industrial plants with mixed nonlinear load</strong> — a combined CHITEK AHF and CHITEK SVG handles both waveform distortion and reactive compensation from one control platform.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Weak-grid feeders</strong> — fast loop response stabilizes voltage where a slow controller would oscillate.</li></ul>
<h2 id="bottom-line" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Bottom Line</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">FPGA control is what lets an active harmonic filter respond within microseconds instead of milliseconds, and it is the architectural reason CHITEK active harmonic filter designs hold THDi below 5% on dynamic loads. The SiC power stage extends that advantage by widening the bandwidth the control loop can actually use. What is your worst-case load step at the PCC — and does your filter's control loop have the latency budget to track it?</p>
